blocking open question: the repl designation
---------------------------------------------

a tool declared in MCP_Repl.thy is INVISIBLE and UNCALLABLE under a repl
designation, and the availability floor does not rescue it after the
move.

  mechanism: Ir builds a repl's base theory with
  \<open>Theory.begin_theory (id, Position.none) thys\<close> (ir.ML:446) over the
  repl's OWN imports — typically just Main. MCP_Repl is not among them,
  so \<open>Ir.context_of\<close> (ir.ML:392), which
  MCP_Protocol.designated_context resolves a "repl:ID" designation to
  (MCP_Repl.thy:27, MCP_Tools.thy:979-992), carries none of MCP_Repl's
  registry data. both halves break, not just listing: tools_body serves
  no row, `exposure` over the empty row set yields an empty map, the raw
  name reaches ml_run, and MCP_Protocol.run_tool's \<open>MCP_Tool.get\<close>
  (MCP_Tools.thy:1077) fails against the repl context.

  why it does not bite today: the mirror rows are equally invisible
  there, but an empty builtins section IS the availability floor — scala
  serves the full builtin table (mcp_server.scala:1363). after the move
  there is no table row to fall back to.

  why the spec's SCOPED block does not cover it: that block narrows the
  floor for "a broken heap or an unresolvable designation". a repl
  designation is neither — it resolves fine, the repl exists, and
  \<open>tool_scope_set {repl}\<close> followed by more repl_step calls is the
  documented self-extension workflow (plans/tool_scope).

candidate resolutions, enumerated but NOT chosen here (this needs a spec
decision from the parent, like the 2026-07-13 activation guardrails):

  (a) resolve a repl designation to the repl context TRANSFERRED to a
      theory merged with the server's own (Theory.merge +
      Proof_Context.transfer). preserves the goal state, keeps one
      registry; cost is a merge per tools/list and an untested
      interaction with repl theories built at runtime.
  (b) a scala-side always-served CORE set: moved tools keep a static
      listing fallback when the designated context serves no row. this
      is the availability floor generalized, and it re-creates exactly
      the duplication the move deletes.
  (c) declare the moved tools somewhere every repl imports. impossible:
      a repl's imports are client-chosen.
  (d) accept the narrowing and document it (repl scope loses the repl
      tools). rejected here on its face — it breaks the primary
      workflow — but recorded so the decision is explicit.

until this is decided, waves 1..6 must not start. steps 1..5 may.

