new fifth form: capture
------------------------

every Ir.* function is WRITELN-STYLE. MCP_Repl.dispatch is typed
\<open>string -> (string * string) list -> unit\<close> and results arrive through
the output channel, while MCP_Tool's run slot returns a string. ml_run
would make ~20 tools hand-roll the capture-and-join-errors block
exec_text (MCP_Tools.thy:617) already implements for diag wraps. so:

  MCP_Combinators.capture:
    string -> MCP_Tool.param list ->
      (Proof.context -> (string * string) list -> unit) -> MCP_Tool.tool

form tag "capture"; MCP_Tool.form grows a Capture constructor beside
String_Fun / Diag_Wrap / Method_Wrap / Builtin. the run slot is

  fn ctxt => fn args =>
    (case MCP_Output.captured (fn () =>
        Print_Mode.with_modes [] (fn () => f ctxt (validate ctxt params args))) of
      (Exn.Res (), output) => output
    | (Exn.Exn exn, output) =>
        if Exn.is_interrupt exn then Exn.reraise exn
        else error (if output = "" then Runtime.exn_message exn
                    else output ^ "\n" ^ Runtime.exn_message exn))

three things are load-bearing and each has a test below:

- PLAIN PRINT MODE, not PIDE. MCP_Repl.fork_run runs under
  \<open>Print_Mode.with_modes [Print_Mode.PIDE]\<close> and the scala ir_result
  handler strips the markup back out
  (\<open>XML.content (YXML.parse_body (YXML.Source (msg.text)))\<close>,
  mcp_session.scala:441). run_tool_result does NOT
  (mcp_session.scala:429, bare \<open>msg.text\<close>), so a capture tool printing
  under PIDE mode would leak yxml to the client. exec_text's
  \<open>Print_Mode.with_modes []\<close> (MCP_Tools.thy:621) is the shape to copy.
- NO BUFFER REGISTRATION ANYWHERE ELSE in the same task tree — see the
  async step below.
- the ARG ACCESSOR. \<open>validate\<close> already guarantees required args present
  and defaults filled, so the lookup can be TOTAL, unlike the
  dispatcher's raw AList.lookup with its own error path
  (MCP_Repl.thy:325). add to MCP_COMBINATORS:

    arg:      (string * string) list -> string -> string
    arg_int:  (string * string) list -> string -> int
    arg_opt:  (string * string) list -> string -> string option   (D3)
    arg_all:  (string * string) list -> string -> string list     (D5)

  `arg` raises an ML error naming the parameter if the key is absent —
  that is a TOOL BUG (a name not in the params clause), not a client
  error, and should read as one.

ANNOTATIONS: capture has NO default. the form tag cannot supply the hint
— Ir.show is read-only, Ir.step mutating, Ir.remove destructive, one
form — so the mcp_tool command REQUIRES an explicit (annotations ...)
clause for the capture form and errors at registration without it
(contrast diag_wrap, where Keyword.is_diag PROVES readOnly+idempotent).
the clause/record mechanism itself is D2's, not this plan's.

async MCP.run_tool
-------------------

ML-only, zero scala change. MCP_Session.ml_run (mcp_session.scala:548)
is already UUID-keyed and promise-based, byte-for-byte the shape ir()
uses (mcp_session.scala:576). the edit is at MCP_Tools.thy:1130.

copy MCP.ir's TWO-FUTURE shape (MCP_Repl.thy:468-490): fork the work,
then fork a SECOND future with \<open>deps = [Future.task_of result]\<close>,
\<open>interrupts = false\<close>, and post \<open>(status, output)\<close> by id from THERE. do
not post from inside the worker body: if the body is interrupted the
scala promise never resolves and tools/call hangs forever — the same
failure mode designated_context_safe's comment records
(MCP_Tools.thy:994-1000).

DO NOT copy fork_run's buffer half. fork_run registers an output buffer
because Ir.* are writeln-style; MCP_Tool.run returns its string and each
moved tool captures for itself via MCP_Output.captured. MCP_Output.
find_buffer (MCP_Tools.thy:288) walks the worker's group ancestry via
\<open>Task_Queue.str_of_groups\<close> and takes the FIRST registered gid found
anywhere in it, so registering at two levels of one task tree is
genuinely ambiguous — the capture inside would or would not see its own
output depending on table order.

CONCURRENCY: tool execution goes serial -> concurrent. the registry is
Synchronized, contexts are immutable, MCP.ir has forked since phase 2,
and Ir serializes per-repl through with_claim — so same-repl contention
is unchanged and what is new is genuine interleaving across DIFFERENT
repls on the run_tool path. treated as an assumption with a
discriminating test (A6), not an aside.

