ASSUMPTIONS -> tests
---------------------

A1  a capture tool returns what its function PRINTED, and joins output
    with the error message on failure — the exec_text contract, at a
    form that returns instead of assembling.
    test [ml unit, MCP_Tools_Tests.thy]: a capture tool over
    \<open>writeln\<close>; one that writelns then errors; one that only errors.

A2  capture's arg accessor is TOTAL for declared params after validate,
    and a lookup of an UNDECLARED name is an ML error naming it (a tool
    bug, distinct from a client error).
    test [ml unit]: required param present; defaulted param absent from
    the call but present in args; \<open>arg args "nope"\<close> errors.

A3  no markup leaks. a capture tool's text arrives at the client with no
    yxml, because run_tool_result does not strip and capture runs in
    plain print mode.
    test [bridge]: tools/call repl_show on a live repl; the result text
    contains no yxml control characters. do NOT also assert equality
    with the isabelle://repl/{id} resource text — that path is
    PIDE-mode-then-stripped and this one is plain-mode, and byte
    equality across the two is a stronger claim than needed (pin it only
    if measured true).

A4  ASYNC run_tool posts exactly once per id, including on interrupt of
    the worker, because the reply rides a dependent future.
    test [bridge]: a slow capture tool and a fast one concurrently — the
    fast reply arrives first, both promises resolve; a cancelled/erroring
    slow call still resolves its promise (no hang).

A5  BUFFER UNAMBIGUITY: run_tool's fork registers NO buffer, so
    MCP_Output.find_buffer resolves to the capture's own group.
    test [ml unit]: a capture tool nested inside an explicitly
    registered outer group sees only its own output; [bridge] as A6.

A6  CONCURRENCY is safe under the new execution model. the
    DISCRIMINATING test is not "slow does not block fast" (MCP.ir
    already proves that) but OUTPUT ISOLATION.
    test [bridge]: two capture-form tools running concurrently against
    two DIFFERENT repls; assert neither result contains any of the
    other's output. this is exactly what a double buffer registration
    over one task tree would break, given find_buffer's ancestry walk.
    second case: registry reads during a concurrent declaration
    (Synchronized) do not tear.

