spec tensions found
--------------------

recorded for folding back, in the "spec refinement:" tradition.

S1 REPL DESIGNATION (blocking; see above). the spec's SCOPED block
   narrows the availability floor only for a broken heap or an
   unresolvable designation. a repl designation is a resolvable one that
   still loses every moved tool, listing and calling both. not covered,
   and it breaks the documented self-extension workflow.

S2 DEFAULT DESIGNATION. the spec never mentions that "" resolves to the
   MCP_Tools theory (MCP_Tools.thy:958) while the moved tools must live
   in MCP_Repl.thy. as written, the move would make them invisible
   out of the box. step 1 fixes it; the spec should record the hook.

S3 find_theorems / find_definition are listed MOVABLE ("logic already
   entirely ML, reached only through Ir"). FALSE as stated: their
   handler_fn normalizes `theory` through resolve_context_theory —
   which the spec's own "retiring MCP.ir" paragraph identifies as
   having no ML equivalent — and errors when repl and theory are both
   given.

S4 the DEFERRED At_Most_One paragraph says these two tools "silently let
   theory win when both are given ... discarding the repl argument with
   no diagnostic", and defers the constructor to avoid a client-visible
   break. that describes the ML DISPATCHER, which clients never reach
   for these two: the scala handler errors first (mcp_server.scala:634,
   :691), and mcp_handler_tests.scala:514/:562 pin the error. so moving
   them as specced would CREATE the break the deferral exists to avoid —
   turning a clean error into silent precedence.

S5 "MCP.ir shrunk from ~28 fnames to 8" undercounts by two. after the
   move TEN dispatcher cases survive: the 8 production ones plus
   \<open>theories\<close> and \<open>load_theory\<close> (MCP_Repl.thy:371-372), which have no
   builtin row, no scala call site, and live test callers —
   mcp_bridge_tests.scala:199 (the load_theory KNOWN GAP regression) and
   MCP_Repl.thy's own build-time self-test. either they are dead weight
   to delete deliberately, or the count is 10; do not leave it implicit.

S6 the spec's INTERFACE PRESERVATION paragraph covers descriptions but
   not ERROR TEXT, which also changes (dispatcher's "MCP.ir: ... for
   \"replay\"" -> validate's "Missing required argument: repl"). same
   verdict (an improvement), same need to be recorded rather than
   discovered.

S7 "ML-only, zero Isabelle/Scala change" is true of async run_tool
   (step 5) but NOT of the migration as a whole: ml_tool_annotations
   (D2) and the zero-param schema branch (step 4) are both scala edits
   without which the first moved tool ships a wrong interface.

